to Garrity's $1.8M and a 43-to-1 advertising advantage", "Garrity's low name recognition, unknown to a third of voters and a quarter of Republicans", "Popular incumbent running in a D-tilted midterm environment"], "rationale": "Every recent poll puts Shapiro up double digits, with the best surveys near twenty points, and his fundraising and advertising dominance has left Garrity largely undefined a month out. Raters unanimously place the race in the Democratic column and markets price Shapiro around 97 percent, supporting a Safe D call.", "watch": "The race could tighten if Garrity gains traction through debates or a late surge of Republican outside spending, or if an unforeseen scandal engulfs the Shapiro administration.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Josh Shapiro (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Josh Shapiro enters the final weeks of his reelection bid in commanding shape. Recent polls, including a late-September New York Times/Siena survey of likely voters, put him ahead of Republican challenger Stacy Garrity by roughly twenty points, and independent averages cluster in the high teens to low twenties. Every major race rating calls Pennsylvania's governorship for Democrats, and betting markets give Shapiro about a 97 percent chance of winning.\n\nThe resource gap explains much of the dynamic. Shapiro raised $13.3 million in the latest period to Garrity's $1.8 million and outspends her on the air by more than forty to one, while polls show a third of Pennsylvania voters, and a quarter of Republicans, cannot yet identify the Republican nominee. Garrity needs a debate moment or an outside spending surge to become competitive; nothing in the current data suggests one is coming.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9929, "margin": 15.66}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9928, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9978, "margin": 19.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9956, "margin": 18.0}]}